## Package comparison

The package remains aligned with approved Phase 3 and this round stays within its server/executable ownership seam. Exact temporary listener cleanup is now implemented, and the foreground executable can race its signal wait against a typed post-bind fault. The phase still lacks required acceptance evidence and one server-lifetime integration remains incomplete.

## Findings

### High — The Phase 3 lifecycle and failure matrix remains substantially unimplemented

The focused suite is unchanged at six facade-level tests and still has no narrow Node-operation/lifecycle-hook constructor or exact counters. There is no deterministic evidence for direct Layer acquisition and two-client replay/broadcast; natural pre/post-hello scope and forwarder finalization; blocked command or blocked sample shutdown; acceptance racing closure; no writes after finalization; injected close/unlink failures and `ENOENT`; cleanup continuation and memoized failed-close results; or signal-handler removal on completion/interruption. The package explicitly makes these acceptance gates for this phase, and the coder result acknowledges they remain outstanding.

### High — Post-bind failure is not owned by every server graph

`awaitFailure` is a lazy service effect. `music-sessiond.ts` now evaluates it in `raceFirst`, but `startMusicSessionServer` never runs it and the Layer itself has no supervised watcher joined to its lifetime. Under the Promise facade, `onServerError` therefore only appends to an unbounded queue; no scope fails or closes and no Promise caller observes the tagged fault. This does not satisfy the package requirement that post-bind listener errors be observed by the server Effect lifetime rather than depending on one particular consumer remembering to run an auxiliary effect. Integrate fault supervision into the common scoped graph/facade and prove it with deterministic injected-error coverage.

### High — Connection defects are recorded but never observed

Connection fibers are added to `FiberSet`, but shutdown only calls `clear` and `awaitEmpty`; it never joins or otherwise observes the set's recorded non-interruption failures. Synchronous encoding/write defects from `send`, for example, can terminate and finalize one connection while their cause remains unobserved. The package requires connection defects to be observed and isolated, not silently detached. Add an observation path that preserves peer/listener isolation and deterministic evidence for a failing socket/write boundary.

## Verification

The reported checks are reproducible: 6 server tests, 65 coordinator/provider tests, and all static scans pass. They do not exercise the remaining gates above.
